{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1301 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "1ad1590fb0e47cec2a9657c33f096a6c023b65e02e04871b30c9ee257ea21711" :edb/db-before-t 1300 :edb/db-after-t 1301 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534613 50 #inst "2026-10-12T04:44:52.371Z" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186164929 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164930 13194139534613 17592186045417 false] [17592186164929 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164931 13194139534613 17592186045417 false] [17592186164929 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164932 13194139534613 17592186045417 false] [17592186164929 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164933 13194139534613 17592186045417 false] [17592186164929 1075 #edb/ref 17592186164934 13194139534613 17592186045417 false] [17592186164929 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427748 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186164929 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427749 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186164929 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427750 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186164929 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427751 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186164929 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427752 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186164929 1078 #edb/ref 17592186427752 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186166567 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,6),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](images/3d/3d7a4a25652434612765ebef26c7c0956342611968008bc4908a4865297e047d.png)\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in(-6,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+10(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-10x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+10(-1)=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534613 17592186045417 false] [17592186166567 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,6),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](images/de/de535d3df741309e51bab7305aedcb27507b078771f0931d4914e16c88220926.svg)\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in(-6,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+10(-x)) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-10x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+10(-1)=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186166568 1071 "Let $F(x)=4x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n![](images/20/20ec903a94cb720f0d2c50b427409e36705af1f9f226b5c6fb11f93f92f0dd49.png)" 13194139534613 17592186045417 false] [17592186166568 1071 "Let $F(x)=4x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n![](images/e2/e25e66141569f5324759eb15a1dcf8b143f5018cf35e957f7a212ba3b45b3556.svg)" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186166991 1050 600.0 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186167284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186167284 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186167284 1071 "$f(x)∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}\\sin (nx)$\nLet $F(x) = 2$ for $x ∈ [0, π)$. The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for $b_{n}$.\n$b_{n} =$ {{field-1}}" 13194139534613 17592186045417 false] [17592186167284 1074 "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{n\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}.\n$$" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186168039 1064 "In the earlier topics, we saw that *symmetry* can force many Fourier coefficients to be zero automatically.\nIn particular, if a $2\\pi$-periodic function is *odd*, then its Fourier series has *sine terms only*.\n\nA **Fourier sine series** uses this idea when a function is initially given only on the half-interval, say, $[0,\\pi),$ but we want a full Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ (and then a $2\\pi$-periodic extension to all real $x$).\n\nTo do this, we start with a function $F(x)$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and define its **odd extension** $f(x)$ to $(-\\pi,\\pi)$ by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, a function $F(x),$ defined on $x \\in [0,1),$ and its odd extension $f(x)$ to $x \\in (-1,1)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f915ed450be1abf672e3fd86b56c02820514a778a4d4d174409f5bd99da6834.png)\n\nBy construction, $f$ is an *odd* function, meaning $f(-x)=-f(x)$. Because $f$ is odd, its Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ has *sine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\\sin(nx)\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is odd and $\\sin(nx)$ is odd for any $n \\geq 1,$ then $f(x)\\sin(nx)$ must be even.\nSo, the sine coefficients can be computed using the *half-range formula*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}(nx)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo in practice, the workflow is the following:\n\n- Start with $F$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and form the odd extension $f$ on $(-\\pi,\\pi).$\n\n- Compute $b_n$ from the half-range integral on $[0,\\pi).$\n\n- Write the Fourier sine series of $f.$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." 13194139534613 17592186045417 false] [17592186168039 1064 "In the earlier topics, we saw that *symmetry* can force many Fourier coefficients to be zero automatically.\nIn particular, if a $2\\pi$-periodic function is *odd*, then its Fourier series has *sine terms only*.\n\nA **Fourier sine series** uses this idea when a function is initially given only on the half-interval, say, $[0,\\pi),$ but we want a full Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ (and then a $2\\pi$-periodic extension to all real $x$).\n\nTo do this, we start with a function $F(x)$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and define its **odd extension** $f(x)$ to $(-\\pi,\\pi)$ by\n![](images/77/776119c1d430a7d50051ae0e00e254f9d22782bc192aa8a0cab9cc227fe460db.svg)\n\nFor example, a function $F(x),$ defined on $x \\in [0,1),$ and its odd extension $f(x)$ to $x \\in (-1,1)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f915ed450be1abf672e3fd86b56c02820514a778a4d4d174409f5bd99da6834.png)\n\nBy construction, $f$ is an *odd* function, meaning $f(-x)=-f(x)$. Because $f$ is odd, its Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ has *sine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} b_n\\sin(nx)\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is odd and $\\sin(nx)$ is odd for any $n \\geq 1,$ then $f(x)\\sin(nx)$ must be even.\nSo, the sine coefficients can be computed using the *half-range formula*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo in practice, the workflow is the following:\n\n1\\. Start with $F$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and form the odd extension $f$ on $(-\\pi,\\pi).$\n\n2\\. Compute $b_n$ from the half-range integral on $[0,\\pi).$\n\n3\\. Write the Fourier sine series of $f.$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186315028 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,5),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,5), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-5,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-5,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(3(-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-(3{x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186315028 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,5),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,5), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-5,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-5,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(3(-x)^{2}-2(-x)) \\\\  & =-(3{x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534613 17592186045417 false] [17592186379568 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+4(-x)-9) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-4x-9) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+4x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186379568 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-((-x)^{2}+4(-x)-9) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-4x-9) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+4x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534613 17592186045417 false] [17592186379582 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-4$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186379582 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-4$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139534613 17592186045417 false] [17592186427748 1080 "\\frac{2(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427748 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427748 1083 #uuid "0288041e-13a5-5541-bbd8-c13988faf24e" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427749 1080 "\\frac{(1-(-1)^{n})}{4\\pi n}" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427749 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427749 1083 #uuid "319ba16a-7d74-5bcd-99b2-bc9850751276" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427750 1080 "\\frac{2(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427750 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427750 1083 #uuid "7a9fb755-7be5-5ae8-be1c-cea88ce12977" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427751 1080 "\\frac{4(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427751 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427751 1083 #uuid "82973565-ede8-565e-a9ef-215aaa0446b5" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427752 1080 "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427752 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534613 17592186045417 true] [17592186427752 1083 #uuid "d703d305-1fef-58e0-ac9f-aedced6da080" 13194139534613 17592186045417 true]] :edb/